//? Title: Slides in mepml
//? Subtitle: A small deck that shows what a slide can hold
//? author: Jordan Schupbach
//? Type: presentation

// A slide is `\slide(` on a line of its own, the slide's content, and a
// line holding just `)`. `//? Type: presentation` above makes the exports
// slide decks: html is a slideshow, tex and pdf are Beamer, and pptx and
// odp are PowerPoint and Impress decks (<leader>ke, or mep-mepml convert).
// A deck holds only what is on a slide, so this comment is left out.

\slide(
> Slides in mepml
Any mepml can go on a slide. The slide's first heading is its title.

- Open with `\slide(` on a line of its own
- Close with `)` on a line of its own
- Everything in between is ordinary mepml
)

\slide(
> Inline formatting
- *Bold*, ~italic~, _underlined_ and =highlighted= text
- |Monospaced|, `verbatim`, ^super^script and ,,sub,,script
- \color(teal, Coloured text) and \fs(16, larger text)
- Links such as [the mepml help|../../../help/mepml.html]
) // a trailing comment on the closer is fine

\slide(
> Lists
1. Numbered items
2. Nest as usual
   - with bullets underneath
   - as deep as you need

- [x] Task lists work
- [ ] and so do open tasks
)

\slide(
> Maths
The linear discriminant maximises the ratio of between-group to within-group
scatter:

$$
J(w) = \frac{w^\top S_B\, w}{w^\top S_W\, w}
$$
\caption(Fisher's criterion)

Inline maths works too: $\Lambda = |W| / |W + B|$.
)

\slide(
> A table
| Species    | Petal length | Petal width |
|:-----------|-------------:|------------:|
| setosa     |         1.46 |        0.25 |
| versicolor |         4.26 |        1.33 |
| virginica  |         5.55 |        2.03 |
\caption(Mean petal size by species, in cm)
)

\slide(
